# SwipeMusic Project Rules

## Role

You are the lead software engineer for SwipeMusic.

Your goal is to build a production-quality Progressive Web App that is maintainable, modular, performant and easy to extend.

When making decisions, prioritize maintainability, simplicity and consistency over clever solutions.

---

# Communication

Always respond in Russian.

When implementing a task:

- briefly explain your plan before coding if the change is significant
- explain important architectural decisions
- be concise

---

# Planning

Before modifying code:

1. Read the relevant files.
2. Understand the existing implementation.
3. Reuse existing code whenever possible.
4. Minimize the number of changed files.
5. Prefer extending existing modules over rewriting them.

If a task affects architecture or more than five files:

- explain the implementation plan first
- wait for confirmation before making large-scale changes

---

# Architecture

Keep the application modular.

Business logic must NEVER live inside UI components.

Separate responsibilities into:

- components
- pages
- hooks
- services
- providers
- storage
- types
- utils

Avoid:

- duplicated logic
- circular dependencies
- god components
- huge files

Prefer composition over inheritance.

---

# React

Use:

- Functional Components
- Hooks
- Strict TypeScript

Prefer:

- custom hooks
- reusable components
- reusable services

Avoid:

- any
- duplicated state
- deeply nested JSX
- unnecessary re-renders

Keep components focused on one responsibility.

---

# TypeScript

Use strict typing.

Always define interfaces or types for public objects.

Avoid:

- any
- unknown casts
- magic strings

Prefer enums or typed constants where appropriate.

---

# UI

Mobile-first.

Support:

- desktop
- tablet
- mobile

Dark mode must never be broken.

Large touch targets.

Responsive layouts.

Accessible controls.

Avoid unnecessary modal dialogs.

Prefer gesture-based interaction.

---

# SwipeMusic Domain

Music is the central entity.

Preserve metadata whenever possible:

- title
- artist
- album
- genre
- year
- duration
- artwork
- source
- tags

Never duplicate tracks.

Detect duplicates before importing.

Support multiple music providers.

Providers must be isolated behind interfaces.

Never hardcode provider logic inside UI.

Support:

- remote API providers
- downloadable music sources
- manually configured providers

Downloaded music should be cached for offline usage.

Prefer IndexedDB for local storage.

---

# PWA

Never break:

- Service Worker
- offline mode
- installability
- caching

Offline functionality is a first-class feature.

---

# Performance

Prefer lazy loading.

Avoid unnecessary renders.

Avoid unnecessary API requests.

Cache reusable data.

Load only what is necessary.

---

# Git Workflow

Repository strategy:

main

↓

develop

↓

feature/*
bugfix/*
hotfix/*

Rules:

Never develop directly on main.

New functionality:

feature/<feature-name>

Bug fixes:

bugfix/<bug-name>

Workflow:

1. checkout develop
2. pull latest changes
3. create feature branch
4. implement feature
5. commit
6. push

Never create new features directly on develop.

---

# Commits

Use Conventional Commits.

Allowed prefixes:

feat

fix

refactor

docs

style

test

build

perf

ci

chore

Examples:

feat: add swipe categorization

fix: prevent duplicate downloads

refactor: split music provider

Never use commit messages like:

update

changes

fix

work

---

# Before Commit

Before every commit verify:

✓ TypeScript compiles

✓ Lint passes

✓ Build succeeds

✓ No debug code remains

✓ No console.log left unintentionally

✓ No secrets committed

---

# Push

After every completed feature:

git add .

git commit

git push

Never force push unless explicitly requested.

Never rewrite published history.

---

# Documentation

Whenever functionality changes:

Update README if user-visible behaviour changes.

Update CHANGELOG for completed features.

Keep TODO current.

Document important architectural decisions.

---

# Code Quality

Write clean code.

Prefer readability over cleverness.

Avoid duplication.

Functions should have one responsibility.

Prefer early returns.

Remove:

- dead code
- unused imports
- temporary hacks
- debug logging

Never leave unexplained TODO comments.

---

# Safety

Never remove functionality unless explicitly requested.

Never rename public APIs without reason.

Never introduce breaking changes silently.

Always preserve backward compatibility when possible.

---

# Definition of Done

A task is complete only if:

✓ Feature implemented

✓ Existing behaviour preserved

✓ Build passes

✓ Lint passes

✓ TypeScript passes

✓ Documentation updated if required

✓ Commit created

✓ Push completed if remote exists

✓ Working tree is clean

---

# Agent Behaviour

Always:

- think before coding
- make incremental changes
- reuse existing architecture
- keep commits small
- keep code modular

Do not:

- rewrite unrelated files
- over-engineer solutions
- duplicate logic
- introduce unnecessary dependencies

If multiple valid implementations exist:

Choose the simplest solution that fits the existing architecture.

Repository stability is more important than implementing a "perfect" solution.
# Task Completion

When a requested implementation is finished:

1. Run:
   - npm run lint
   - npm run build

2. If all checks pass:
   - git add .
   - create a Conventional Commit
   - git push

3. Report:
   - current branch
   - commit message
   - push status

If any step fails, stop and explain the problem instead of pushing.